<p>
  The <strong>Company secretarial</strong>
  module keeps the statutory records a Kenyan company must maintain under the Companies Act, 2015,
  and reminds you of what has to be filed with the Registrar of Companies (on e-Citizen / BRS).
</p>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  The deadlines below are common defaults to help you stay organised, not legal advice. Confirm the
  rules that apply to your company with your company secretary or advocate.
</.callout>

<h2>1. Company details</h2>
<p>
  Open <.link navigate="/compliance">Company secretarial</.link>
  and enter the registration number, company type, date of incorporation, KRA PIN, registered office
  and financial year end. If the company must have a secretary (public companies, and private
  companies with paid-up capital of KES 5 million or more), tick that box so Zippiker reminds you.
</p>

<h2>2. Registers</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Register</th><th>What you record</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><.link navigate="/compliance/officers">Directors &amp; secretaries</.link></td><td>
        Appointments and cessations, with ID, KRA PIN, nationality and residential and service addresses.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><.link navigate="/compliance/members">Members &amp; shares</.link></td><td>
        Share classes, members, allotments and transfers. Holdings are worked out from the movements.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><.link navigate="/compliance/beneficial-owners">Beneficial owners</.link></td><td>
        Individuals with 10% or more of shares or votes, the right to appoint or remove a director, or significant influence or control.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><.link navigate="/compliance/charges">Charges</.link></td><td>
        Security over company assets: lender, property, amount, registration and satisfaction.
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Every register has <strong>Print register</strong> for a signed hard copy or PDF.</p>

<h2>3. Filing calendar</h2>
<p>
  The <.link navigate="/compliance/filings">filing calendar</.link> fills itself:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Annual return</strong>: each year, due 30 days after the anniversary of incorporation.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Director or secretary changes</strong>
    and a new <strong>registered office</strong>: notify the Registrar within 14 days.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Allotment of shares</strong>, <strong>beneficial ownership</strong>
    changes and new <strong>charges</strong>: within 30 days.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Special resolutions</strong> passed at a held meeting: file within 30 days.</li>
  <li>
    Public companies: the <strong>AGM</strong> within six months after the financial year end.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  When you've filed, click <strong>Mark filed</strong>
  and note the e-Citizen receipt number. Overdue and due-soon items show on the module's overview.
  You can add your own deadlines too.
</p>

<h2>4. Meetings and documents</h2>
<p>
  Record board meetings, AGMs, EGMs and written resolutions with their agenda, attendance, minutes and
  resolutions, and print the minutes for signing. Keep the certificate of incorporation, articles,
  CR12 searches and filing receipts under <.link navigate="/compliance/documents">Documents</.link>.
</p>
